   * scripts/eval_correction_loop.py::_seed_correction (~L136)
       Same 6-column shape, inside a rolled-back transaction.
   * scripts/verify_chancery8.py (L210/223/240) — three seeds.

  READERS
   * services/correction_retrieval.py::get_relevant_corrections
       - kind='classification' (~L85): filters c.org_id = $1 AND
         c.field_name = 'doc_category'; LEFT JOIN LATERAL document_extractions
         ON document_id = c.document_id AND org_id = c.org_id.
       - kind='extraction' (~L124): JOIN document_template_extractions dte
         ON dte.id = c.template_extraction_id AND dte.org_id = c.org_id,
         filters c.org_id = $1 AND dte.template_type = $2.
   * scripts/verify_chancery6.py L263, L355, L429 — count/select by document_id.
   * scripts/verify_chancery8.py L520 — count by org.

  CONSUMERS (indirect, via correction_retrieval)
   * services/document_classifier.py L170 — few-shot injection, org_id from caller.
   * services/textract_extraction.py L315 — deterministic K-1 post-map override.

  DOES ANY CALL SITE ASSUME org_id IS ALWAYS PRESENT?
    get_relevant_corrections opens with `if org_id is None: return []` — an
    explicit NULL check — and then filters `c.org_id = $1` in the SQL. This is
    the load-bearing fact for this sprint: an org_id IS NULL row can never
    satisfy that predicate, so the new global rows are invisible to the document
    retrieval path BY CONSTRUCTION, not merely by RLS policy. No call site
    dereferences org_id in a way that breaks when the COLUMN becomes nullable,
    because every writer still supplies it.

